Z||000000||paulg||04-26-99||06:22 PM||||My 86 se needs an engine. Should I put in a stock engine, modify? What are the pros and cons? I'm not a mechanic. Can you recomend a shop in the north jersey area?
Z||000001||theogre||04-26-99||07:20 PM||||it all depends on what your end goal is.<p>mine for example is generally to stay close to stock, even with the 4 cyl motor. I do that for 2 reasons. 1. I always know what parts fit it, and so does anyone I may sell a car too. (tho I usually keep a car untill it's scrap) 2. I like the stock performance. It meets my needs. My 4 cyl 87SC goes 0-60+ fast enough to clear an off ramp more than safely, and I get enough gas milage to have my work pay for most of my repairs. (I get X cents per mile from work. more than it cost me to drive for all my driving. my last expence check is paying the insurance this month.) I'm esentially driving a free car that meets every need I have for a car.<p>could I make my car go faster, yes, a hell of allot faster in fact. I'd also have so many tickets right now I'd probly lose my license.
Z||000002||Ross||04-26-99||08:05 PM||||Hy..ogre is you'r car an SE or SC? 
Z||000003||theogre||04-27-99||07:23 PM||||87 4cyl SC. It has the stiffer suspension, but it's coded diferant than WS6. That I can tell so far WS6, and the "heavy duty" setup I have are the same. BTW.... If your car came with 14"AL wheels in 87, the HD susp was a GM required option. I don't know if that's true for other years, but that's how the codes broke down when I did them awhile back.
